      "content": "When assessing risks in African American and Middle Eastern/North African communities, officers should be aware of the following:\n\nIn African American communities, assertiveness can sometimes manifest as direct confrontation or tough-talking, which can escalate quickly. Physical postures like standing with feet shoulder-width apart, direct eye contact, and raised voices can be red flags. Additionally, power poses like standing with fists clenched or making expansive gestures can indicate underlying tension. \n\nIn Middle Eastern/North African communities, assertiveness may take the form of verbal arguments, righteous indignation, or stern tone. Officers should look out for stalling or evasion tactics, verbal posturing, and assertive gestures like sweeping motions with the arm. Specifically, any behavior that crosses personal boundaries or shows a deep-seated sense of injustice may be a sign of escalating tension.",
